## §1 — OBSERVE

- **Queue state (read this cycle):** QUEUE.md recreation section at Wave 36 close: DESCRIBED-OWED rows #104–#105 = T233 · T238; T233 stands at row #104, status DESCRIBED/OWED, first attempt owned by this job (A3 §3). Wave RUN.md §0b/§1 concurs.
- **Consumer (named, §1.4):** the **DLC assembler transition container** — the substitution-at-action transition surface owned by the Daily Language Challenge assembler lane: an action spans the swap, the incoming object's identity is the action's expected product (packet §6 lesson L1 names this container class for exactly this grammar).

### 1.1 Catalogue mechanics (read firsthand)
- Digest §T10 (39–46 s, clip clips/pincushion-grid.mp4 41–46 s): "A tomato-red pincushion is de-pinned (its pins read as the tomato's stem-points), set into a grid cutter, and pressed through; what emerges is a slab of translucent red cubes — diced tomato — gathered by hand and added to the molcajete. The transformation is performed by a real kitchen tool doing its real job; the substitution rides inside the press's occlusion." Why it works: "the tool's function is the argument — a grid cutter can only output cubes, so the output's identity is forced by the instrument."
- Style-library techniques-transitions.md §T233: agrees verbatim on mechanics, window (P13 @ 39–46 s), evidence (ev/t041 · ev/t044 · clip). Tier DESCRIBED — OWED #104. No digest↔register disagreement on window or evidence.
- **Firsthand corrections to the digest prose (priced readings, binding on the build):** (1) The visible de-stemming is the removal of the green felt STEM STAR (census CP1→CP2), not pin removal — the pearl-head pins REMAIN in the cushion through the press (visible in CP3/CP4 through the grid cells); digest B5's "de-pinning … fumbled" prices the same beat's low legibility. The build implements stem-star removal + pins retained. (2) The emerged "translucent red cubes" are literally translucent RED DICE with white pips (ev/t044 + census CP5 viewed firsthand: 3×3 slab, two layers, white pips on every top face) — the dice read as diced tomato; the build renders pipped cubes with `pipStyle` CONFIG-bound (dice/plain), it does not render unmarked tomato cubes. (3) The clip file covers only 41–46 s; the full window was censused from the archived `source.mp4` (VP9 1920×1080, 100.894 s, ffprobe this run) — the archived source segment WAS watched in motion via the frame census below (this seat has no player; the census is the motion record, A10.3 form).

